      "source": [
        "**Your answers:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. We use bidirectional LSTM so the model can read the sentence both forward and backward. The forward pass captures past context (what came before a word), while the backward pass captures future context (what comes after). This is important because meaning in language often depends on both sides of a word. For example, the word “bank” means something different depending on the words around it. Using both directions improves understanding and accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. The embedding layer converts each word into a dense vector that represents its meaning. Unlike one-hot encoding, which treats all words as unrelated, embeddings place similar words closer together in vector space. This allows the model to understand relationships like “king” and “queen” or “car” and “vehicle.” It also reduces dimensionality, making training more efficient.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Concatenation keeps all information from both forward and backward passes. If we added them, we would combine the values and potentially lose important distinctions between directions. By concatenating, the model gets a richer representation of the sequence."
      ]
    },
